course: dictionary
lesson: 8180
title: Respectable and what it tells us
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words describe how a person behaves, and others describe how something looks or how good it is. Respectable is both. It means good, honest and proper, and it can also mean neat enough to be seen in public.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «You're the very image of her ... only I'm sure you never looked half so respectable in all your life before.» The girl is fastening a gown and teasing, so respectable here is about looking neat and smart. Try your meaning in the sentence: "neat and proper" still makes sense.
explain: Respectable can also mean fairly good, though not excellent. A «respectable mark» in a test is not the top mark, but it is good. Ask yourself: would "good, though not top" fit here? If it does, you have found the meaning.
fact: Respectable means good, honest and proper in the way you live and behave.
fact: «Respectable» can mean decent and good enough to be seen, as in «Put on something respectable for the wedding.»
fact: «A respectable score» or «a respectable sum of money» means fairly good or fairly large, though not excellent.
fact: In stories about Victorian and Edwardian England, a respectable family had a clean house, went to church and behaved properly.
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, Toad's wild driving and stealing a car make him far from respectable.
exercise: «Put on something respectable for the wedding.» What does respectable mean here? | Torn and muddy | Very loud | *Decent and good enough to be seen | Far away
exercise: In a test, Ella gets «a respectable mark». What does this suggest? | A mark with no meaning | The worst mark in the class | Her test was cancelled | *Good, though not top
exercise: In Victorian stories, why did a family want to be respectable? | To win every race in town | *To have a clean house, go to church and behave properly | To hide from the neighbours | To drive wild cars and steal them
test: In The Wind in the Willows, Toad's stealing a car makes him far from respectable. What does this suggest? | Very quiet and shy | Very tall and strong | Very neat and proper | *Not at all proper or honest
test: «A respectable distance» means | *A fair, sensible distance, not too close | Exactly one metre | Right next to you | Very, very far away on a map
exam: «You're the very image of her ... only I'm sure you never looked half so respectable in all your life before.» In this sentence, respectable means | Rude and loud | *Neat and proper, good enough to be seen | Sad and tired | Wet and muddy
